al's  poisoning back in 2018  of course that's what people will think  of when they hear the word  novichok it's a soviet era nerve agent  and it was found to have been used  on sergey skripal a former russian  a former russian spy and his daughter in  south west england  two years ago now they were both ill  but in fact the um substance ended up  killing a member of the public  who happens to find a container  containing some of the leftover  uh agent uh just uh if you like on the  streets  uh and all along russia uh denied any  involvement in that even though the  british authorities actually  identified the two russian men who they  think  actually carried out the attack and it  caused a lot of  uh alarm almost panic particularly in  the city of salisbury  where the attack happened but because  for months the authorities couldn't tell  people  exactly what had happened and where the  substance had gone  so yeah it really is a worrying  situation the dean baba  in london for us thank you so much  nadine well let's now move over  to alexandre stroyanovich godfor to get  the view from moscow  alexandra this has all happened i know  just over the last hour or so but i see  we are already getting some pushback  from the kremlin  yes exactly whilst the german reaction  was fifth year reactions are  cautious and restrained in a way  so ministry of foreign affairs responded  by saying that russia is still waiting  for a german prosecutor general office  to respond to their request  and their request was to send all the  results of the analysis that  were done to navalny also to state for  instance what was the level of his  cholinesterase  in the plane so before he arrived into  germany and german clinic  wanting all these details um and  a member of a russian parliament he's  actually ahead of the russian  committee for foreign affairs in the  parliament he said that russia is very  willing to cooperate  but these statements coming from germany  have to be followed with some solid and  concrete  proof so the situation now is that from  the authorities the reaction is still  restrained but we heard before  that the russian doctors found no poison  they actually released navalny  with the diagnosis of metabolic disorder  disorder of  carbohydrates and that two labs in in  russia  didn't find anything suspicious and the  investigation  which is actually in a pre-investigative  state didn't found  any reason to think of any any foul play  on the other hand opposition is uh  saying in one voice  we knew it all the symptoms were there  and  a friend of navalny and his colleague  evangelist  tweeted that using novichok is actually  like signing the crime uh saying that it  can only be used by  certain state organizations namely by  for instance military intelligence or  the state security service so the  opposition  is pointing fingers and they're pointing  fingers at the authorities well i want  to ask you more about that alexander  because as nadine was saying  the novichok group of agents was what  was used on sergey skripal  back in 2018 and then it was linked to  the military intelligence agency the gru  so does that now make it  more difficult for the kremlin to  distance itself from this  well yes it does but if we go back to  the skripal case uh  even though scotland identified two  suspects  and journalists also identified them as  members of the military intelligence  russia denied it they said these are two  russian tourists they were completely  innocent they were sightseeing in  salisbury  we saw um in famous interview with this  two people  uh claiming they have nothing to do with  anything and this  is a sort of a pattern that we are  seeing that  when some other state accuses russia of  some  power play of poisoning their opponents  or double agents like in a case of  script  russia is saying it is not us or there  is no  evidence and you have to provide us with  solid proof  and what you are giving us is not solid  proof